  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1144 PM CDT THU SEP 24 2026\n\nClouds linger across the southeast this evening but should\ngradually clear, though stubborn cloud cover may persist south of\nI-85 through Friday morning. With clearing skies and cooler\ntemperatures, patchy fog will be possible, especially near\nwaterways, potentially impacting the morning commute. Morning\ntemperatures are expected to be 5 to 10 degrees cooler than\n24 hours ago. Persistent northerly flow will continue advecting\ncool, dry air through the weekend, pushing low temperatures to\ntheir lowest point Saturday morning, with mid 50s northeast to\nlower 60s southwest.\n\nThe forecast remains dry and cool through Sunday, with heat \nindices close to the actual temperatures. Model guidance begins \nto diverge by Monday, exhibiting a slower trend with the next \nfrontal boundary. A weak trough and surface low pressure system \nwill track eastward next week, bringing the next precipitation \nchances. Given the slower movement, rain is expected to enter the \nstate around mid- week or slightly later.\n\nSoutherly begins to increase slowly by Tuesday and Wednesday.\nThis return flow will drive a gradual warming trend and slow\nmoisture recovery, although we may remain dry at least through\nThursday morning. Unfortunately it does not look like we have see\nthe end of the upper 80s to low 90s as models are hinting at\nanother round of warm temperatures to usher in October.\n\n16",
